  "question": {
    "text": "For coagulation of arsenious sulphide sol, which of the following salt solutions will be most effective?"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow><mi>O</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><mi>N</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><mi>B</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "According to Hardy&#39;s -schulz rule, greater the valency of the active ion or flocculating ion greater will be its coagulating power. <math><msup><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> is <math><mo>-</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></math> charged colloid so cation having more <math><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></math> charge, more will be coagulating power.<br /><br />Hence <math><mi>A</mi><msup><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math> having maximum coagulating power"
  }
}